00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.980
SCOTT JESCHONEK: So Avere's
around nine years old-- eight,

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.120
nine years old, and, you
know, when we originally

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.750
started the product
line and the company,

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.882
it was to address performance
and latency issues with network

00:07:35.882 --> 00:07:36.590
attached storage.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:37.760
So if you were--

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.160
let's say you were
creating a movie

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.490
and you wanted that
movie to be rendered

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.730
in a certain period of time and
you created a render farm, you

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:49.870
have, whatever, a thousand
servers sitting there, well,

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:52.370
those servers are going to beat
the crap out of your network

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:54.950
attached storage, it's
inevitable, especially back

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:56.720
then because flash
was still really

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.940
expensive and hard to really
come by for those solutions.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.520
Over time, we evolved
our solution off

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:07.160
of our data center
product and started

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.320
adopting cloud technologies.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.500
Specifically, if you wanted
to create, for example,

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.264
a render farm in
the cloud and you

00:08:14.264 --> 00:08:15.680
wanted to orchestrate
that and run

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.920
that against some data that's
sitting wherever, Avere

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.990
provides the technology,
the caching technology,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.110
data access file
technology for that.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:27.830
But we also support
abstracting a POSIX file system

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:30.080
on top of Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.221
So if you have applications
that you want to run

00:08:33.221 --> 00:08:35.220
and you want to put all
the data up in the cloud

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.370
but you really need
those POSIX bits

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.980
and you need to manage it as
you did before, Avere gives you

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:41.179
that continuity.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:44.450
MARK MANDEL: So is this
just a cloud solution?

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.140
