00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.563
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Great question.

00:08:49.563 --> 00:08:51.380
So it can be both.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:56.390
We have customers today, very
large studios in Hollywood

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:00.440
that do a lot of rendering
and it's all on-premise.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.410
But we have other
customers in that space who

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.990
have started to break up
their data center clusters

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:10.142
and are looking
at running in GCP.

00:09:10.142 --> 00:09:12.350
A lot of the reason that
they want to do that is it's

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.100
far more flexible.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.157
They don't have to own so much
hardware on-prem, especially--

00:09:17.157 --> 00:09:18.740
like, a lot of these
guys have sort of

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.450
capped out as to
how much they really

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.781
want to put in a data
center, so having GCP,

00:09:23.781 --> 00:09:26.030
especially being able to
take their existing workflows

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.620
and putting them up there
is a real advantage.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:32.180
So we run both on-prem and
we ported our appliance

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:38.990
into GCE instances that can run
in GCE, adjacent to whatever

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.383
workload that you're running.

00:09:40.383 --> 00:09:41.150
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:44.360
It sounds like it's either
on-prem or on Google Cloud.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.069
Is that the case?

00:09:45.069 --> 00:09:46.318
SCOTT JESCHONEK: That's right.

00:09:46.318 --> 00:09:46.884
Or both.

00:09:46.884 --> 00:09:48.050
You can plug them into both.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.330
We're like the Swiss army
knife of data access,

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:54.320
basically, when it comes
to that kind of thing.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.520
DAVE ELLIOT: Yeah that's part
of the beauty of what they offer

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.020
and how they help our
customers approach

