If you think of your
compute instances

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:08.300
in the cloud, your
compute instances on-prem,

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.990
your storage in the cloud--
say with GCS-- and your storage

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.420
on-prem, what Avere
helps companies do

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:19.990
is sort of move between those
four entities, those four types

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:21.689
of compute and storage.

00:10:21.689 --> 00:10:23.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
We're saying that we

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:26.730
are able to have storage
that is distributed

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.980
in between the cloud
and on-premise,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:33.250
does that mean that you
connect in through a VPN?

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.440
Does everything need to
be in the same network?

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:36.650
How does this work?

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:38.608
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:10:38.608 --> 00:10:40.480
So if I were truly a
developer on our team,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:42.040
I would say it depends.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:44.380
And it depends on all
the variables, right?

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.990
So typical use case
is going to be,

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:48.490
we're going to
recommend that you're

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.330
using a gigabit per second
link over the network.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:54.289
Because a lot of
our customer base,

00:10:54.289 --> 00:10:56.080
they're not doing really
small-scale stuff,

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.360
they're doing some
pretty serious uploading

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:01.120
and processing of data,
so laws of physics,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.830
you want to make
sure that data gets

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:04.450
there as quickly as you can.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:07.600
VPN over the internet
is a little tricky

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.440
because we're then at the
mercy of whatever latencies

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:12.670
the internet provides.

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:15.940
One of the things that our
product really strives to do

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:19.700
is be a killer of latency
when it comes to workloads.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.990
So for any 1,000
VMs in GCE, they

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:26.720
can't afford to have latency
bogging down the job.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.860
And so we typically want to
have a link behind us that

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.580
is sufficient enough to get
as much data into our layer

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.570
as we can.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:36.600
Oh, and you also
asked about network.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.860
So like from a GCE
perspective, you're

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:42.700
going to run the Avere cluster,
and we only run cluster.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:45.400
You're going to run
that in a subnetwork--

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.070
not a classic subnetwork,
but a regular subnetwork

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.890
in GCE with your compute nodes.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.709
MARK MANDEL: And does Avere like
encrypt the traffic through?

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:56.000
Or how does-- what is the
security side of things here?

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.624
SCOTT JESCHONEK:
Well, so it's twofold.

00:11:57.624 --> 00:12:00.755
One, if you're using us as a
data access layer with your NAS

00:12:00.755 --> 00:12:03.910
back on-prem, so that's one
of the typical use cases,

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.400
is I may have a NetApp
or an Isilon environment

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.179
and I just need to get to
some subset of that data,

00:12:09.179 --> 00:12:10.720
we're going to tell
you-- and we have

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:12.280
written this all up
in best practices--

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:13.738
that you're going
to want to secure

00:12:13.738 --> 00:12:17.120
your link between your data
center and the Google network.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.120
And it wouldn't matter
if you were just using us

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.430
or if you were using any
of the Google services

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:23.122
that require that
kind of interaction.

00:12:23.122 --> 00:12:24.580
And we don't encrypt
that, we would

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.746
expect that to be encrypted.

00:12:25.746 --> 00:12:30.820
But since we also can use
GCS as backing storage, which

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.740
is kind of an interesting
twist, we basically

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.330
treat GCS as block--

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:35.890
we become the NAS for that--

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.350
that's all SSL encrypted,
as well as the data

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:39.444
being encrypted at REST.

00:12:39.444 --> 00:12:40.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:45.130
So here at the podcast, we are
firm believers in open source,

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.209
so I'd like to know--
is this open source?

00:12:47.209 --> 00:12:49.000
Is there is something
that we can check out

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.040
the code and stuff?

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:52.570
SCOTT JESCHONEK:
Open source, being

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.730
a vendor, such a struggle.

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:57.610
So, you know, look, I'll
tell you what we did do.

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:00.460
With one of the things about our
products, since it's clustered,

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:04.150
you know, the core code itself
is proprietary, because it's

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:07.030
highly tuned for caching.

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.580
We do a lot of complex read
ahead and write behind.

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:11.650
I always kind of
liken it to like

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:15.509
putting rocks in a raging river
and trying to steer the data

00:13:15.509 --> 00:13:17.050
or steer the water
in a certain ways.

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:18.674
It's very difficult
to do and you never

00:13:18.674 --> 00:13:21.020
know what's going to
happen downstream,

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:23.230
so that's really
complicated stuff.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.429
But to manage and
set up the cluster,

00:13:25.429 --> 00:13:27.220
especially if you're
a developer and you're

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.560
looking to create like an
orchestration pipeline,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.405
we created a Python library
that we've put up in GitHub,

00:13:33.405 --> 00:13:35.690
we'll give you the URL
to it, it's no problem.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:39.160
And as long as you are able
to access our image, which

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.670
as simple as reaching out
to Avere and asking us,

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.270
we can grant you evaluation
access to the code.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:48.850
And this Python library,
you can pick your poison.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:51.460
You can either import it
into your Python logic--

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:54.550
that's fine-- and create
clusters of our product

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:57.429
pointing at your network
attached storage back on-prem

00:13:57.429 --> 00:13:59.470
as long as you have the
IP address of the export,

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:00.470
you don't even have to--

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.330
this can all be
done very easily.

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:04.090
And we also created
a wrapper Python

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.574
for that, so basically
with one line,

00:14:06.574 --> 00:14:08.740
you can set up the entire
cluster, fully configured,

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:09.490
ready to go.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:12.760
DAVE ELLIOT: I will say
from a Google perspective,

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.880
you have some options if you're
looking at file services.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.710
You can use our
single node filer.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:19.210
There's some
limitations-- actually,

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.160
a fair number of
limitations on that.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.540
You could also--
we support cluster,

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.736
so if you truly are looking
for an open source solution,

00:14:26.736 --> 00:14:28.360
some of the challenges
there are really

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.690
around configuration
management of large clusters.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:33.970
Or you can use a
commercial partner.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:37.642
Avere is our go-to partner
for these file services.

00:14:37.642 --> 00:14:38.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.770
We actually discussed
on Episode--

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:43.080
let me try to remember--

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.000
Episode 40-- that was
a very long time ago.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.790
We interviewed Rodeo FX and
they were talking about how

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:50.380
they were using GlusterFS.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.720
Would you say that
Avere is kind of like

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:56.869
more managed and all ready
to use version of GlusterFS?

00:14:56.869 --> 00:14:57.910
What are the differences?

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:58.951
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Oh yeah.

00:14:58.951 --> 00:15:01.150
So I'm going to make
it a hypothetical,

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:04.090
even though what I'm saying is
directly related to a customer,

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:05.500
OK?

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.221
SCOTT JESCHONEK: So we
now have a customer,

00:15:08.221 --> 00:15:11.680
they're in the render space,
they build with Gluster.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.800
The reason they did it, which is
a reason a lot of folks do it,

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:15.520
is--

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.100
well first of all, you
know, if you're a developer

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:19.930
and you're good at
what you do, you just

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:22.050
want to get what you
need and move on.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.050
And that's one of the
advantages of open source,

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:26.965
it's like, go get Gluster code
and go run it and I'm done.

00:15:26.965 --> 00:15:29.590
But it's never as simple as that
when you start to introduce it

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.530
into a complex environment like
a render environment, where

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:36.190
latency is an issue, long-term
maintenance is the issue,

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:39.340
you're getting chased by
angry artists and angry studio

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:43.240
people that want, you know,
the latest scene rendered now

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:45.580
and there's an issue
with Gluster open source

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.230
that you can't figure out.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.260
So, you know, you have to take
ownership of a lot of stuff

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:50.800
there.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:52.930
The question that
I always put back--

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:56.050
and this will sound sales-y for
the developers, I apologize,

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.120
I'm not in sales--

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.080
is, that your core business?

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:02.500
Is storage and performance of
storage your core business?

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.300
No, it's rendering movies.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:05.930
So the faster you
can render a movie,

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:08.180
whether you're using commercial
products, open source,

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:10.940
or a combination of it, the
better off for the company.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:14.699
So I would say where we're
different is first of all,

00:16:14.699 --> 00:16:16.240
you can have a
cluster of our product

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:19.480
up and running in about eight,
nine minutes using that Python

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.910
code ready to go serving ops.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:26.470
You can plug in storage from
on-prem, which with Gluster

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:28.040
you really kind
of can't do that.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:29.290
You can have a mount
point, but you're

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:31.540
going to be at the mercy of
whatever the latencies are

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.080
of that link.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:37.330
And then ultimately with
Gluster, you know, is it HA?

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:38.965
Are you going to have
redundant storage

00:16:38.965 --> 00:16:42.430
and then your costs are going
to go up from what you think

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:45.340
is going to be a relatively
inexpensive solution.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:49.840
Once you start trying to
scale it with 500, 600 VMs,

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:52.999
you're quickly going to find
that Gluster just can't keep up

00:16:52.999 --> 00:16:54.790
and you're going to
need something-- either

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:57.340
you're going to have to
build on top of Gluster

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.560
your own custom environment,
which again puts you

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:02.921
in a different business, or you
look for a commercial provider.

00:17:02.921 --> 00:17:03.670
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.430
So let's swing back around to
sort of developer experience

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:07.599
and how that works.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:08.890
So I'm kind of curious as well.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:11.594
So you say there's
a POSIX file system.

00:17:11.594 --> 00:17:13.510
So does-- all right, so
there's two questions.

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:15.069
One, so this
essentially means that I

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:17.619
can interact with this once
it's all mounted and set up.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.430
Basically like a
local file system

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.197
and then you'll handle
all the latency?

00:17:21.197 --> 00:17:23.530
And the other question I'll
tack onto that is, does this

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:24.529
work in both directions?

00:17:24.529 --> 00:17:27.790
Can I go from cloud to prem
and then prem to cloud?

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:28.954
Like, how does that work?

00:17:28.954 --> 00:17:30.120
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Absolutely.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.890
So to answer your first
question, so from a developer

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:35.320
perspective, you know,
you have Google Cloud

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:38.290
Platform with a lot of
fantastic technologies,

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:40.480
like all the machine
learning and TensorFlow

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:42.820
and the micro services
capabilities, that's

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.570
all exciting and it's
sort of like you're

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.080
starting to think
about, how can I

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.480
develop with all these
new tools and what value

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.080
can I drive in the company
with these new tools?

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:54.010
And that's great.

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:56.510
And at your next cycle, that's
what you're building towards.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.020
But then you have this whole
host of applications today--

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:01.330
and I'm going to exclude
database applications just

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:04.660
because if I were using
a relational database,

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:07.360
I'd go put it up in Google's
relational database product

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.750
and be done with it just
to make my life simpler--

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.600
but let's just think about
file-based applications

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:14.200
that you have.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:17.050
So let's say their business,
you know, mission critical,

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:19.400
can't really tear them
down and reauthor them.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.210
They're six, seven years old
and they've just been working,

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.840
do you really want
to break that?

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:25.660
Well, you know, why do that?

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.140
Just go run it up
in Google, run it

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.930
on some the VMs
or some containers

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:33.980
and we just present
a mount point.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:36.869
So you just mount that export
and you're ready to go.

00:18:36.869 --> 00:18:39.160
That's the easiest way to
look at it from a developer's

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:42.190
perspective is, do I want
to spend time rewriting

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:44.920
a lot of things that I
don't need to and therefore

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.730
delay my entry into cloud
because of all of that?

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.580
Or do I want to just slam
them up there into the cloud,

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:53.712
use the exports from
the Avere, and then I

00:18:53.712 --> 00:18:55.920
can go work on these other
new and interesting things

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:58.240
while that stuff's just
doing what it used to do?

00:18:58.240 --> 00:18:59.440
MARK MANDEL: And to the
second question, which

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:01.000
is can I go in both directions?

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.666
SCOTT JESCHONEK:
Thanks for reminding me

00:19:02.666 --> 00:19:05.060
about the second question,
which I totally forgot about.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.920
Yeah, so let's say you're
using our physical appliance.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.560
What are the use cases
with our product?

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:13.930
Especially in life
sciences, so like all

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:17.020
the genetic
sequencing companies,

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:21.360
they're creating amazing amounts
of data-- petabytes of data,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.029
they've got to put it somewhere.

00:19:23.029 --> 00:19:24.820
And so they're all
looking at putting it up

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.560
into places like
Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:27.940
but the problem
that they all run

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:31.090
into, especially in
that particular space,

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:33.100
is all their pipeline
applications,

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:37.330
everything they use to do
alignment and variant analysis

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.040
and everything,
they're all file-based.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:40.730
People are rewriting
some of them,

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:42.470
but it's the same problem again.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:43.570
Is that my day job?

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:47.455
Do I have time to stop
people using the classic tool

00:19:47.455 --> 00:19:49.330
to get this new tool
and then I have to spend

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.220
six months debugging it?

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:52.960
Or can I just put
the stuff up there?

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.590
And so Avere allows to put
it into Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:57.770
but we put it
there in a way that

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:00.360
allows you to see it as POSIX.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:04.180
And when I say POSIX, it
means a directory structure,

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:09.200
the standard POSIX bits, and
we also do it in a sharded way

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:14.780
so that you don't have to worry
about multi-part gets and puts

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.090
or having to get the
entire file out of Google

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:18.320
and pay the egress charge.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:19.236
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:19.236 --> 00:20:21.940
So I don't know
if it's my jet lag

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:24.020
or it's just like
I've missed something,

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:26.630
but I'm curious about
where do you actually

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.250
store this information?

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.646
Is it stored in GCE instances?

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:34.130
Is it stored in Cloud
Storage and persistent disks?

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.724
Where does this information
actually live at the end?

00:20:36.724 --> 00:20:38.390
SCOTT JESCHONEK: It
is not your jet lag.

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.460
It's a completely legitimate
question everybody asks us.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.760
So one of the things
to think of us as--

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:45.950
and when people ask
us what we really do,

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:49.130
I say we basically
facilitate access to data

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.070
and we do it at a high
performance level, all right?

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:55.400
The data itself
resides on your NAS.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:57.770
It resides in your GCS bucket.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.290
The only data that
resides on Avere

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:02.540
is any data that
has been requested

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:04.820
by clients for the latest job.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:06.950
So it's basically a cache.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:10.750
We also leverage
local SSD technology

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:14.450
in Google, which, by the way,
I mean, Google's local SSD

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:17.990
facility is fantastic, we get
great performance out of it.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:20.960
We also do support
the persistent SSDs,

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.670
but you get an upper
bound of throughput

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.540
on the persistent SSDs, so if
we want to max out performance,

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:28.040
we use local.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:30.300
But again, it's
only transient data.

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:31.700
It goes away when you're done.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:32.450
DAVE ELLIOT: Yeah.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:34.340
I'll say, you know,
Avere Systems,

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:38.990
these are file system experts,
and the caching algorithms

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:41.390
that they've developed
over their seven years

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:43.070
are unbelievable.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:44.830
Best of breed or best around.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:48.120
So, you know, it's
a scenario of,

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:51.710
you know, because they've
got these fantastic caching

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:54.810
algorithms, it makes
latency and performance

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:55.784
just that much better.

00:21:55.784 --> 00:21:56.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.310
So my next question
is, since Mark

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:01.100
was asking about the
directions of the mappings

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:04.430
and they either can basically
get like Google Cloud

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:08.360
Storage as POSIX
systems on my instance,

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:13.640
can I also map the same folders
into multiple instances?

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:14.460
Like, is--

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:15.500
MARK MANDEL: Like
multi-read, multi-write?

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:15.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:17.840
Is Avere a good way
of communicating

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.450
across many different machines?

00:22:20.450 --> 00:22:23.030
Or is it more a place where
you basically just use it

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:27.032
to store stuff that is being
used by one single worker?

00:22:27.032 --> 00:22:28.490
SCOTT JESCHONEK:
Ah, good question.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:31.250
The sweet spot use
case if you think

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:33.800
about it is, I'm going
to run a render farm,

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:37.890
I'm going to run a POST
sequence in genomic analysis

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.080
to cure some disease.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:42.950
I'm going to do a
Monte Carlo simulation

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:45.230
in financial services so
I squeeze more money out

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:46.520
of my fund.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.490
Those are all things that
run thousands of machines,

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:51.680
and typically those machines
are going to come up

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:54.440
and they're going to
all read some sort

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:58.440
of common set of data, and that
is tailor-made for caching.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.550
So our-- where we really shine
in terms of technology is

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:05.090
if you have an HPC cluster
or a compute environment

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.090
and you've got a lot of nodes
demanding data from some

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:11.000
storage that is simply not going
to be able to keep up with it,

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:12.770
we do support writing
the data out--

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:15.740
so you can do your render and
then write out your results--

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:17.700
and we cache those
rights as well

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.110
and that gives you performance
on that side of it.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:24.570
From the sort of single
client access in a folder kind

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.170
to use case that you
mentioned, that's

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:30.920
really like the domain of
something like a single node.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:33.440
I mean, it just
depends on how you're

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:35.277
going to use that
particular technology.

00:23:35.277 --> 00:23:37.610
It's not that we can't support
a single client doing it,

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:40.740
it's just that we're really
built for the high performance

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:41.279
space.

00:23:41.279 --> 00:23:43.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just to
dig a little bit deeper,

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:44.900
let's imagine that
we have, as you

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.330
were saying, a lot of
workers that are actually

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:50.870
within the same piece of
data, and then somehow there's

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:53.066
another worker that dates that.

00:23:53.066 --> 00:23:54.440
What you're saying
is that you're

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:57.380
going to manage the caching
validation across all

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.410
of the places where
that information might

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:01.827
have been cached and that will
be transparent to the user?

00:24:01.827 --> 00:24:02.743
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Yeah.

00:24:02.743 --> 00:24:05.360
And just to be clear, that's
within one of our clusters.

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:08.330
So a cluster can be three
nodes and three-node cluster

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:14.200
can serve as, I don't know, 500
n1-highmem-4's or highmem-8's,

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.590
so it's not a small environment.

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:21.840
But you can run 20 nodes, 23
nodes, 24 nodes of our system,

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.552
and that is a tremendously
large scale operation

00:24:24.552 --> 00:24:25.760
that you'd be running there--

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.770
65, 70,000 cores.

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:30.620
But every right gets
cached and every read

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:32.630
gets cached for all the clients.

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:34.130
And the writes are
always protected,

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:36.830
so if somebody writes
something and somebody else

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:39.140
wants to read it, they're
going to read the latest.

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:42.300
If somebody tries to write
on that write, then that--

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.090
that'll all be
tracked and modified

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:45.830
and updated accordingly.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:46.580
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:47.810
I have no idea what the
answer to this question

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:49.143
is going to be, but I'm curious.

00:24:49.143 --> 00:24:51.080
So we're big fans of
Kubernetes and it's

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:53.230
been a hot topic about
how to store our data

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:55.750
inside particularly
containers in Kubernetes.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.210
Is there a plug-in
or basically a way

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:00.730
for using of your technology
to do like persistent volume

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:02.810
claims or basically
store lots of data

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:05.230
so you can do like HPC
workloads on Kubernetes?

00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:06.146
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Yeah.

00:25:06.146 --> 00:25:08.000
I mean, so I looked into this.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.100
I also looked into
Hadoop, right?

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:11.410
And if you're not--

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:13.470
just to diverge for one
second from Kubernetes,

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.460
if you're using
Hadoop and you're not

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:18.340
married to Hbase and other--

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:22.060
like hQuery as part of your
sort of tool chain and you'd

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:25.932
be able to use your Hadoop
job as a file-based job,

00:25:25.932 --> 00:25:28.390
you can actually do something
I call a hybrid Hadoop, which

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:30.830
is basically run a
Hadoop cluster in GCE,

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:34.090
use Avere access layer
against your NetApp.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:37.300
On Kubernetes, I mean,
I'm a little torn,

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:41.260
because in terms of Kubernetes,
if you have a tiny small data

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:44.650
slice and you're a very
sophisticated developer

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:48.090
and you're able to place
that little slice of data

00:25:48.090 --> 00:25:52.617
just so in each container,
you know, that's really fine.

00:25:52.617 --> 00:25:54.200
I mean, you can go
ahead and use that.

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:58.570
But if you want, you can
use Kubernetes containers

00:25:58.570 --> 00:26:01.280
with an export, with an
NFS export mount point.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:04.120
So there's no reason why
you couldn't bring up

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:07.270
lots of containers
pointing at an export

00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:10.365
and consume the data from
that export just as you--

00:26:10.365 --> 00:26:11.740
MARK MANDEL: So
that's actually--

00:26:11.740 --> 00:26:13.450
you hit the lead there a little.

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.211
So you also not only
got your own sort

00:26:15.211 --> 00:26:17.210
of maybe FUSE technology
or something like that,

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:20.200
but you expose it as something
like NFS or other standards,

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:21.899
sort of file mounting
solutions, so

00:26:21.899 --> 00:26:23.440
that if you have
existing tools, it's

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.944
super easy to just get those
up and running with Avere.

00:26:25.944 --> 00:26:26.860
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Yeah.

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:29.068
Actually, you just raised
a critical point too-- this

00:26:29.068 --> 00:26:30.520
is not FUSE.

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:34.917
So FUSE is a little tricky,
because it's user space,

00:26:34.917 --> 00:26:36.500
it's got an upper
bounded performance.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:38.470
If you just need to do
some basic stuff with FUSE,

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:39.970
I'm never going to
argue against it,

00:26:39.970 --> 00:26:44.260
but it's not really meant
for high performance

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:46.420
sophisticated workloads.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.160
It is-- you know,
the stuff we wrote

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.640
is grounded in very,
very fundamental code

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:55.150
that I, you know, unless we all
mutually have signed some NDA,

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:57.949
I can't really say what it is.

00:26:57.949 --> 00:27:00.490
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it sounded
like you had like an NFS mount.

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:03.010
Basically standard file
sharing type protocols

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:04.260
seem to be supported by Avere.

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:05.176
SCOTT JESCHONEK: Yeah.

00:27:05.176 --> 00:27:09.790
So the front end of our product
basically presents NFS or CIFS.

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:13.930
And if you're using GCS object
as the backing storage for it,

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:17.410
it can actually support
multi-protocol out the front.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:21.010
And it integrates with Active
Directory and everything else.

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:23.500
DAVE ELLIOT: So yeah,
Mark maybe we did bury

00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:24.820
the lead a little bit on there.

