00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.190
But we also work in
the oil and gas space,

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:39.520
so you've got a lot of
geologic analysis surveys

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:41.650
that are very read intensive.

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:44.620
You'll hear me emphasize read,
but just so that I'm clear,

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.450
we also have ingest use cases.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:51.640
So for example, somebody I
cannot possibly name nor ever

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:57.640
will name somewhere in the
eastern part of this country

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:01.540
takes a lot of pictures, and
those pictures are being stored

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:04.270
in very deep storage.

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:07.510
And that ingest has to happen
at a certain high rate--

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:11.020
and we're talking about larger
files here so its clear.

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:14.260
And so, you know, especially
on our physical product,

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:17.050
we do a lot of write caching
and we have NVRAM cards

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:18.580
built into our hardware.

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:23.980
So were able to more quickly
ingest and acknowledge those

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:25.072
writes quickly.

00:29:25.072 --> 00:29:27.280
And that's another important
point from a developer's

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.770
perspective-- if you
have a pipeline that

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:34.480
depends on receiving an ACK
from the protocol level,

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.960
Avere does the ACK
immediately after receiving

00:29:37.960 --> 00:29:41.680
the write and cashes that
write across partners.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:46.150
So let's see-- video ingest,
transcoding of video,

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:50.230
