00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:36.300
in the cloud using
Container Builder,

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:37.762
but I don't know
anything about it.

00:33:37.762 --> 00:33:38.970
Like, where do I get started?

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:40.845
What can I do to learn
more about this thing?

00:33:40.845 --> 00:33:41.800
It seems really cool.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:42.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:44.681
So the question was really cool
because the question actually

00:33:44.681 --> 00:33:46.710
comes from someone
yesterday at the Go

00:33:46.710 --> 00:33:49.004
Meetup that took place
in the Docker office

00:33:49.004 --> 00:33:49.920
here in San Francisco.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:50.460
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And they
were talking about the newest

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:53.220
features of Docker.

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:56.040
And one of the cool things
that are coming but still

00:33:56.040 --> 00:34:00.595
not in the stable release
is multistage builders.

00:34:00.595 --> 00:34:02.970
And multistage builders are
super cool because they allow

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:04.678
you to basically do
the typical things we

00:34:04.678 --> 00:34:09.060
do with a makefile, which is
you use a container with all

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:12.420
of your compilers and
all the developing tools,

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:15.360
and then out of that, you
generate a binary that then you

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:16.820
put in a different container.

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:18.940
And normally, we'll use
it to double the makefile.

00:34:18.940 --> 00:34:20.679
At least I'm sure
that you, Mark--

00:34:20.679 --> 00:34:22.199
MARK MANDEL: --I'm going to do
this in a makefile, yes, yes.

00:34:22.199 --> 00:34:23.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: For sure.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:26.449
So now you can do that
with just one Docker file,

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:27.330
which is very useful.

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:29.790
And they asked me, so
how do you use this?

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:31.620
Like, do you upgrade
to the latest version?

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:33.239
And my answer was
like, actually no.

00:34:33.239 --> 00:34:34.770
Actually you can
use this directly

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.260
on Container Builder, which
is super cool, because first

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:39.600
of all, you don't
make your machine work

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:41.820
too much because
the building process

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.040
itself will happen in
the cloud, but also you

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:45.750
get the newest
features of Docker

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:47.639
before they're
actually on stable,

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:50.876
because they basically pick
the best features in order

00:34:50.876 --> 00:34:53.000
to have them there so you
can experiment with them,

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:54.230
which is really cool.

00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:57.110
So the question after
that was, OK, cool--

00:34:57.110 --> 00:34:58.280
how do I use this, right?

00:34:58.280 --> 00:34:59.540
How do they get started?

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:02.540
And of course, the first
answer is, read the docs,

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:03.920
read the manual.

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:07.970
And we have a link to the
docs, but if I not mistaken,

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:11.720
is cloud.google.com/container
builder.

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:14.330
But anyway, we'll have a
link in the show notes.

