I discovered because someone

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:41.960
sent it to me via Twitter,
was that our dear friend

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:45.830
and coworker Carter Morgan
has this series on YouTube

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:47.120
on Container Builder.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.730
And there's two videos already
and they're really good.

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:51.650
The first one is
basically an intro

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:55.130
to Container Builder plus
how to do cloud rolling

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:58.850
updates directly from Container
Builder, which is super useful.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:03.470
And the second one is on how
to use not multistage builds,

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:06.827
but multistep builds, which
is not exactly the same thing.

00:36:06.827 --> 00:36:08.410
Yeah, it's not exactly
the same thing,

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:09.785
it's basically
more like when you

00:36:09.785 --> 00:36:13.820
want to go vet and go test
and then go build and then

00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:16.940
send that to a container and
then that container should be

00:36:16.940 --> 00:36:18.320
deployed somewhere else, right?

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:20.900
Those are multiple steps that
are not necessarily related

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:21.980
to Docker.

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:24.740
Multistage build is
inside of a Docker file.

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.140
So not completely the
same, but very related.

00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:27.950
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:29.780
Now I really-- I love
Container Builder.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:31.790
For a long time I was
